**1a. `src/lib/ai/assumption-watch.server.ts:136-152` — OPEN.** Up to 10 assumptions per tick, each doing a serial AI judge call *and* a serial `UPDATE`:

```ts
for (const a of rows) {
  const verdict = await judgeAssumption(supabase, userId, workspaceId, a.statement, evidence);
  await supabase.from("assumptions").update({ last_watched_at: nowIso }).eq("id", a.id);
  if (!verdict.contradicted || verdict.evidenceIndex === null) continue;
  const { error } = await supabase.from("assumption_challenges").insert({ ... });
}
```
`judgeAssumption` takes `evidence` as a parameter (no shared mutable state across iterations), so this is safe to run concurrently and batch-write:

```ts
const verdicts = await Promise.all(
  rows.map((a) => judgeAssumption(supabase, userId, workspaceId, a.statement, evidence)),
);
await supabase.from("assumptions")
  .update({ last_watched_at: nowIso })
  .in("id", rows.map((a) => a.id));

const challengeRows = rows
  .map((a, i) => ({ a, v: verdicts[i] }))
  .filter(({ v }) => v.contradicted && v.evidenceIndex !== null)
  .map(({ a, v }) => ({ workspace_id: workspaceId, assumption_id: a.id, ... }));
if (challengeRows.length) await supabase.from("assumption_challenges").insert(challengeRows);
```
Cap is only 10 rows so latency impact is modest today, but it scales linearly with watch-list size and doubles as unnecessary DB round trips.

**1b. `src/lib/meetings.functions.ts:192-199` — OPEN.** After a proper batch-insert of decisions, stage history is written one row at a time (up to 15 iterations, each its own network round trip):

```ts
for (const d of insertedDecisions ?? []) {
  await recordStageEvent(supabase, { entityType: "decision", entityId: d.id, from: null, to: "pending", ... });
}
```
`recordStageEvent` (`src/lib/stage-events.server.ts:44`) does a single-row `insert` into `stage_events` — trivially batchable:
```ts
const stageRows = (insertedDecisions ?? []).map((d) => ({
  entity_type: "decision", entity_id: d.id, from_stage: null, to_stage: "pending",
  actor: "human", workspace_id: d.workspace_id, user_id: userId,
}));
if (stageRows.length) await supabase.from("stage_events").insert(stageRows);
```

**1c. `src/lib/ai/eval-runner.server.ts:148-236` — PARTIALLY OPEN, and a stale fix-claim.** DB writes here *are* correctly batched (result rows collected into `resultRows` and inserted once after the loop — confirmed at line 226). But the AI calls themselves are fully serial: two `await callModel(...)` per case, one case after another, with no concurrency. A session buffer note claimed "concurrency limiter" was added for this — it is not in the file (no `Promise.all`, `p-limit`, or any concurrency helper). For a 20-case suite that's 40 sequential round trips to the model provider. Fix with a bounded-concurrency map:
```ts
async function mapLimit<T, R>(items: T[], limit: number, fn: (item: T) => Promise<R>): Promise<R[]> {
  const results: R[] = new Array(items.length);
  let i = 0;
  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: limit }, async () => {
    while (i < items.length) {
      const idx = i++;
      results[idx] = await fn(items[idx]);
    }
  }));
  return results;
}
// then: await mapLimit(cases, 4, async (c) => { ...existing per-case body... })
```

**1d. `src/lib/ai/supersession.server.ts:110-140` — lower priority.** Per-edge sequential `upsert` + conditional invalidate `update` inside a loop. The upsert half is easily hoisted to one batched `upsert(edges[], {...})` before the loop; the invalidate half is conditional per-row so is harder to batch cleanly — worth doing only if `selected.length` regularly exceeds a handful.

**Already fixed (verified, not re-flagged):** `src/lib/lineage.functions.ts:353-374` (`promotePrdToTasks`) batch-upserts lineage edges after task insert — matches the earlier fix claim, confirmed live. `src/lib/linear.functions.ts:287-290` batches `sync_mappings` inserts with an explicit "Phase 3: 1 round trip instead of N" comment.

